Term	Description
Repository	A repository (or repo) is the central location where all project files and their complete version history are stored.
Branch	A branch is an isolated line of development that allows changes to be made independently of the main code base.
Commit	A commit records a set of related changes in the local repository. Each commit represents a logical development step and creates a new version in the project's history.
Fetch	Fetch downloads the latest changes from the remote repository without modifying the local working branch. It updates the local view of the remote repository.
Push	Push uploads local commits to the remote repository, making them available to other team members.
Pull Request	A Pull Request (PR) is a request to review and merge the changes from one branch into another, typically the main branch.
Merge	Merge combines the changes from one branch into another. After a successful merge, the target branch contains all approved changes.

Ich würde noch drei weitere Begriffe aufnehmen, da sie im Workflow ebenfalls vorkommen:

Term	Description
Checkout	Checkout switches the local working copy to a specific branch. All subsequent changes are made in the selected branch.
Main Branch	The main branch is the primary branch of the repository. It contains the approved and integrated version of the project.
Work Item	A Work Item represents a task, bug, or user story in Azure DevOps. It is used to plan, track, and link development activities to the corresponding source code changes.

